Hi—I have data on annual tree growth, annual precipitation, and long-term average precipitation from 58 sites. I have estimated the coefficient of the annual growth-precipitation relationship, and for each site, I would like to show two things:

a) the growth-precipitation coefficient (scatterplot with one dot per site)
b) the long-term average precipitation (single-row “heatmap”, or grid plot, with color indicating normal precipitation)

I have produced each of these plots separately in ggplot using the attached data.
library(ggplot2)

plots.fits <- read.csv("plots_fits.csv",header=TRUE)

## Order sites ("plots") (for x-axis) by geographic cluster and then by decreasing long-term average precipitation
plot.levels <- unique(plots.fits$plot.id <http://plot.id>[order(plots.fits$cluster,-plots.fits$ppt.normal)])
plots.fits$plot.id <http://plot.id> <- factor(plots.fits$plot.id <http://plot.id>,levels=plot.levels)

## first plot (scatterplot)
ggplot(plots.fits,aes(x=plot.id <http://plot.id>,y=ppt.coef.mean,colour=ppt.normal)) +
  geom_point(aes(size=ppt.r.sq)) +  #make size of point proportional to R-sq of linear model fit
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin=ppt.coef.mean-ppt.coef.stderr,ymax=ppt.coef.mean+ppt.coef.stderr))+
  facet_grid(. ~ cluster,scales="free_x",space="free_x") + #plot separate facet for each geographic region, or "cluster"

## second plot ("heatmap" of long-term average precipitation)
ggplot(plots.fits,aes(x=plot.id <http://plot.id>,y=1,fill=ppt.normal)) +
  geom_tile(stat="identity") +
  facet_grid(. ~ cluster,scales="free_x",space="free_x")+
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90,hjust=1,vjust=0.5))

But what I really want is something like this:

Ugly photoshopping, but you get the idea.

It this possible in ggplot2 or any other R package(s)?

Thanks!

Derek

